Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.855
So, every year, I let my students talk about the work that we've done during the year.

00:00:03.855 --> 00:00:07.440
So then, I'm left with just talking about whatever's on my mind.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.910
So today, so this year,

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:09.990
I thought, uh, initially,

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.415
I was going to talk about some, um,

00:00:11.415 --> 00:00:13.275
some new results in, uh,

00:00:13.275 --> 00:00:15.750
cryptocurrencies but then I looked at the program and we actually

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.465
have a fair number of cryptocurrency,

00:00:18.465 --> 00:00:20.010
uh, talks coming up already,

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:21.240
including the very next talk.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.460
So, what I decided to do is talk about something

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.890
that's kind of been botherin- bothering me lately,

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:27.580
uh, which is this,

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:29.850
this issue of crypto implementations.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.970
In particular- particularly bugs in crypto- crypto implementations.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.370
Okay, so let me explain what I mean by that,

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:36.780
uh, and to do that, first of all,

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.480
I have to give a little bit of background.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:41.640
So, uh, let's start with the kind of the basic,

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:42.995
basic problem in cryptography.

00:00:42.995 --> 00:00:44.280
Right. So the basic, very,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.250
very basic problem is basically we have two users,

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:50.730
you know, we can't give a crypto talk and not mention Alice and Bob.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:52.740
So, we have our two users, Alice and Bob,

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.000
and they have a shared key,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.310
and Alice wants to send a secure message to Bob.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:57.570
How does she do that?

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:00.105
Yeah, this is like the crypto 101 problem.

00:01:00.105 --> 00:01:01.650
The simplest, simplest, uh,

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.320
aspect of crypto you can imagine, and you ask people,

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.300
well how do you do this and,

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.225
um, you'd be amazed on how many different answers you get.

00:01:09.225 --> 00:01:12.290
Well, the correct answer is this notion called,

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:14.280
uh, authenticated encryption, yeah,

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.350
which takes a bit of work to define what

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:19.305
authenticated encryption actually means, but informally,

00:01:19.305 --> 00:01:22.800
what authenticated encryption means is the following: there should be some notion of

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.065
confidentiality so an adversary who sees many messages can't figure out, um,

00:01:28.065 --> 00:01:32.250
any other information about plain texts from messages of our choice and there has to

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:36.240
be a notion of integrity which is that even though I can send you lots of,

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:41.250
um, uh, sorry, even if I can see lots of messages that Alice sends to Bob,

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.805
the adversary cannot change the data that's em- embedded in these messages.

00:01:44.805 --> 00:01:46.350
Yeah. So, one is confidentiality,

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:47.695
the oth- other one is integrity.

00:01:47.695 --> 00:01:51.390
So that's, a- and again, there's a very formal definition that goes into, uh,

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:54.615
defining this precisely which I'm not going to torture you with, uh,

00:01:54.615 --> 00:01:56.640
here, although I listed some papers,

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.495
uh, if you want to learn more about this.

00:01:58.495 --> 00:02:01.620
So what are the constructions for, for authenticated encryption?

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.275
Really, that's kind of a canonical construction,

00:02:04.275 --> 00:02:09.650
it's called a generic composition of an encryption mechanism and an integrity mechanism.

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:10.980
Yeah, so this is called, uh,

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:14.910
encrypt-then-MAC and the way it works is you have your plaintext,

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:17.340
uh, you encrypt the plaintext to get

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:21.175
a ciphertext and then you compute an integrity tag on the ciphertext.

00:02:21.175 --> 00:02:22.800
Okay, so, uh, the MAC,

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.750
which is what's computing the integrity tag,

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.700
is being applied to the ciphertext and that's

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.800
the thing that's being transmitted over the wire.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.495
Okay, so you can see why it's called

00:02:30.495 --> 00:02:34.440
encrypt-then-MAC because first you encrypt and then you MAC the message.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.910
Uh, so here, I wrote up the algorithm very simply, you know,

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:40.370
you encrypt your message m using the encryption key,

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:43.500
you compute a MAC of the ciphertext using the MAC key,

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.320
and then you output the ciphertext and the integrity tag.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:47.835
Very simple, yes.

00:02:47.835 --> 00:02:51.045
Uh, and then the way you de- the way you decrypt is you just do the obvious thing,

00:02:51.045 --> 00:02:52.215
you check the MAC.

00:02:52.215 --> 00:02:54.480
If that doesn't verify you output bottom,

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.040
else, you output the decryption.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.750
Yeah, nothing could be simpler,

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.640
and in fact, there are standards that do this.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.745
Uh, there's a well-known standard called Galois counter mode,

00:03:02.745 --> 00:03:04.860
this is now required in TLS 1.3.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:09.090
In fact, TLS 1.3 is one of the big developments in crypto of this year.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:11.055
TLS 1.3 was just ratified.

00:03:11.055 --> 00:03:13.950
It's a much, much, much nicer protocol than,

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:17.430
um, than, uh, earlier versions of TLS.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.770
It's going to be widely available, widely deployed now,

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:21.420
and it's a big deal in the,

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.955
in the world of, uh, crypto in this, in this past year.

00:03:23.955 --> 00:03:25.650
And in fact, um, uh,

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:30.150
the required method of encrypting in TLS 1.3 is using this GCM method,

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.040
although there are other methods that are supported as well.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.180
And the way that works is basically you encrypt using something called counter mode,

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.365
which we'll see later,

00:03:37.365 --> 00:03:40.095
and then you compute a MAC using something called a Carter-Wegman-MAC,

00:03:40.095 --> 00:03:41.535
which is a very fast MAC.

00:03:41.535 --> 00:03:43.515
Okay, good. Uh, so,

00:03:43.515 --> 00:03:46.155
so very simple, what could possibly, uh, go wrong?

00:03:46.155 --> 00:03:48.690
Uh, although I have to tell you that there is more, uh,

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:51.930
bells and whistles to authenticate an encryption just beyond,

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.790
um, you know, taking a key and a message.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.790
Uh, in fact, there are other properties of authenticated encryption.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:59.985
For example, maybe you want to have something called associated data

00:03:59.985 --> 00:04:03.150
which is data for which you can compute integrity,

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:04.845
but you don't actually encrypt.

00:04:04.845 --> 00:04:08.100
Yes, so think of like packet headers in a packet, you don'-,

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.880
you want to encrypt the packet data but you don't want to encrypt the packet headers,

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.400
so the headers will be ca- called associated data,

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:15.795
uh, and so, you know,

00:04:15.795 --> 00:04:18.540
they basically the associated data feeds into the, into the tag,

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.580
into the integrity tag but itself,

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:22.335
it's not actually encrypted.

00:04:22.335 --> 00:04:24.890
That's one property you'd like to have as, uh,

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:28.740
as authenticated encryption with associated data, it's called AEAD.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:32.010
And you'd like, you'd also like to basically avoid randomness, you know,

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:35.070
cryptographic libraries should not be generating randomness,

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:36.435
so you should be taking, uh,

00:04:36.435 --> 00:04:38.970
these nonces, uh, from the outside.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:41.040
Uh, and so, you'd like to encrypt using what's,

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:42.375
what's called the unique nonce,

00:04:42.375 --> 00:04:44.040
so a nonce that does not repeat.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:46.770
Uh, and if you do that, that's called nAEAD,

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:49.710
so nonce-based Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:52.020
You see why we like acronyms, it's a lot of words.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.390
And the way the algorithm works, again, very simple.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:57.990
Uh, basically you encrypt the message using the nonce that you were given,

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:00.480
and then you compute the integrity tag over

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:04.680
the ciphertext and the associated data also using the nonce that you were given.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:06.315
Okay? All very simple,

00:05:06.315 --> 00:05:08.310
uh, what could possibly go wrong?

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:10.260
All right. So, that's how we, how we do,

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:12.045
uh, encryption in the real world.

00:05:12.045 --> 00:05:13.380
We have these, um,

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:15.450
uh, yeah, we, uh, compute encrypt,

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.370
we encrypt the computer MAC, all,

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:19.545
all done using associated data and nonces.

00:05:19.545 --> 00:05:21.660
It turns out there are even more properties you could

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.820
ask for, for authenticated encryption.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:25.005
So, for example, uh,

00:05:25.005 --> 00:05:27.450
I told you that these nonces better not repeat.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:28.670
Well, in the real world,

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:29.795
it turns out, uh,

00:05:29.795 --> 00:05:33.000
there are lots of examples where nonces accidentally do repeat.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:37.185
For example, if you have a nonce implement as a counter and you reset your device,

00:05:37.185 --> 00:05:39.940
somehow the nonce resets to zero and then all of a sudden,

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:42.300
you're re-encrypting multiple messages using the same,

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.265
uh, using the same value of the nonce.

00:05:44.265 --> 00:05:45.960
So, there's this, uh, requirement,

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:49.290
there's a desire to have what's called nonce-misuse resistance.

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:54.270
This is called Misuse Resistance Authenticated Encryption, MR- MRAE.

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:59.055
Uh, and so the idea is that there is some security even if the nonce is repeated,

00:05:59.055 --> 00:06:00.480
and what I mean by some security,

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:01.645
I mean, that you ha-,

00:06:01.645 --> 00:06:04.630
you still have security as long as the messages are all distinct.

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:06.450
Yeah, as long as you're not encrypting

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:09.165
the same message with the same nonce, everything is still fine.

00:06:09.165 --> 00:06:11.270
Yeah, so we even have ways of doing that,

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:14.415
there's a very famous mode called Synthetic IV mode,

00:06:14.415 --> 00:06:16.980
SIV, um, which I'll show you in a second,

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:20.295
which actually, uh, achieves misuse resistance as well.

00:06:20.295 --> 00:06:24.020
Then you might want to require something like online encryption and decryption,

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:26.010
so online authenticated encryption.

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:30.430
So here, what happens is you'd like to be able to decrypt blocks of the ciphertext

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:34.875
and release blocks one by one before you have seen the entire, entire ciphertext.

00:06:34.875 --> 00:06:37.480
Yeah. So normally, you would collect the entire packet,

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:38.530
decrypt the whole thing as a whole,

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:40.080
as a whole, but in fact,

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:42.855
there are many cases where you'd like to release, uh,

00:06:42.855 --> 00:06:45.920
so your block might spend multiple packets,

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:49.070
but packets might come in one by one and you'd like to be able to release

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:52.845
packets as they come in rather than waiting for the entire block to come in.

00:06:52.845 --> 00:06:55.575
So, this is called online authenticated encryption,

00:06:55.575 --> 00:06:57.120
that's a little problematic to define,

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.250
but we even have ways of, of doing that.

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:01.440
Yeah, so there's a lot of the story keeps, uh,

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.060
keeps on going and there are more requirements

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:05.295
for authenticated encryption you can imagine.

00:07:05.295 --> 00:07:07.620
Now, it turns out there are lots of ways to

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:09.810
build authenticated encryption, so I just put a,

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:13.800
a picture here from a paper, uh,

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.080
by, uh, um, um, uh, Namprempre, Rogaway,

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:19.350
and Shrimpton that shows you a whole bunch of constructions for authenticated encryption.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.430
Now, it doesn't really matter what this slide is,

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:23.380
um, I wanted to just mention quickly that,

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:25.590
I just wanted to show you that there are lots of ways to build them,

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:26.820
I want to mention that this,

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:27.970
uh, SIV method that I,

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:30.270
I talked about is this picture over here,

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:31.800
which what happens is you basically,

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:36.240
you can see you encrypt your message but the randomness, the IV,

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:38.640
the randomness that you use to encrypt your message is

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.130
derived as a function of the nonce,

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:43.010
the message, and the associated data.

00:07:43.010 --> 00:07:44.175
Yeah. So, that's kind of the,

00:07:44.175 --> 00:07:46.545
you see the ciphertext and the tag coming out.

00:07:46.545 --> 00:07:49.170
This is a mode that's actually nonce-misuse resistance.

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:52.890
Even if you do encrypt using the same nonce but with different messages, you see,

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:56.700
you end up with different IVs and that's why the mode still provides security.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:08:00.300
Okay. So, this was just a quick overview of authentic- authenticated encryption.

