And then, remember, we have to do encrypt then sign.

00:22:50.125 --> 00:22:53.280
Okay? But the, but then what happened was the,

00:22:53.280 --> 00:22:57.520
the entire ciphertext was then signed. All right.

00:22:57.520 --> 00:22:59.080
So what is the problem with this?

00:22:59.080 --> 00:23:00.710
The problem here is,

00:23:00.710 --> 00:23:03.900
remember I told you that you can do encrypt then sign

00:23:03.900 --> 00:23:07.180
as long as the underlying encryption primitive is secure.

00:23:07.180 --> 00:23:11.295
The underlying encryption primitive needs to provide authenticated encryption.

00:23:11.295 --> 00:23:12.850
But let's think back.

00:23:12.850 --> 00:23:16.920
Authenticated encryption means that you have to encrypt then compute

00:23:16.920 --> 00:23:21.395
a MAC then compute an integrity checksum and you have to sign the whole thing.

00:23:21.395 --> 00:23:23.780
That's again you you were supposed to do encrypt,

00:23:23.780 --> 00:23:25.755
MAC, and sign the whole thing.

00:23:25.755 --> 00:23:29.015
And the logic, I- I imagine the logic that was kind of, um,

00:23:29.015 --> 00:23:31.040
used in deriving this mode is to say,

00:23:31.040 --> 00:23:33.360
"Look, the whole data is already signed."

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:35.195
If the whole data is signed,

00:23:35.195 --> 00:23:36.790
we don't need a MAC on the data.

00:23:36.790 --> 00:23:38.770
We don't need an additional integrity checksum.

00:23:38.770 --> 00:23:42.930
Yeah, it looks like the signature provides the integrity checksum that we,

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:45.135
um, that we wanted, um, for integrity.

00:23:45.135 --> 00:23:47.070
Yeah, so the point was, um,

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:50.540
the signature provides authentication so there's no need for a MAC.

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:52.190
Yeah, so, we can simplify, uh,

00:23:52.190 --> 00:23:53.310
and get rid of the MAC.

00:23:53.310 --> 00:23:54.635
Yeah, sounds reasonable.

00:23:54.635 --> 00:23:56.285
Signature provides integrity.

00:23:56.285 --> 00:23:58.400
Why do we need to, to have a MAC as well?

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:01.215
Why do we have a separate integrity check, checksum?

00:24:01.215 --> 00:24:02.855
Well, it turns out, um,

00:24:02.855 --> 00:24:05.660
this is completely broken.

00:24:05.660 --> 00:24:07.530
Yeah. So this has to be fixed.

00:24:07.530 --> 00:24:08.880
