00:24:25.625 --> 00:24:27.050
So we have this, uh,

00:24:27.050 --> 00:24:29.995
we have a ciphertext and we have a signature on the ciphertext.

00:24:29.995 --> 00:24:32.675
What the attacker is going to do is the following.

00:24:32.675 --> 00:24:35.825
The difference between a signature and a MAC is fundamental.

00:24:35.825 --> 00:24:39.385
Right? A MAC, I can only compute if I know the secret key.

00:24:39.385 --> 00:24:43.180
A signature, I can compute with any signing key that I want.

00:24:43.180 --> 00:24:46.070
So what I'm going to do as an attacker, I just, you know,

00:24:46.070 --> 00:24:49.070
I- I- I intercepted a message,

00:24:49.070 --> 00:24:50.765
encrypted message that you sent.

00:24:50.765 --> 00:24:53.220
As an attacker, what I'm going to do is I'm going to remove

00:24:53.220 --> 00:24:56.060
the signature that you sent and instead,

00:24:56.060 --> 00:24:57.855
I'm going to put in my own signature.

00:24:57.855 --> 00:25:00.460
Okay? I'm going to put in my own signature.

00:25:00.460 --> 00:25:04.805
So, this is a signature you see by using the attacker's secret key.

00:25:04.805 --> 00:25:06.530
So far- so far so good.

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:10.870
Now, the attackers are going to send this whole ciphertext over to the server.

